# Target C-II — a minimal one-anchor `K_5` dual block produces a six-point unit-force reciprocal equilibrium

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proved candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the degree-pure five-face endpoint of the proof-only one-anchor closure-corank reduction  
**Depends on:** one-anchor transpose-kernel theorem; its `K_5` minimal-support consequence; bounded contact-pentagon no-parallel-side lemma; exact stopped-train occurrence signs  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The one-anchor corank theorem leaves a sharp finite dual object: five degree-five
one-anchor residual faces whose nonparallel relation graph is `K_5` and which support a
nonzero pure-contact dual closure mode.  This note converts that object into an exact
six-point reciprocal equilibrium problem.

The conversion is stronger than a direction-only restatement.  If the five dual face
vectors are `y_1,...,y_5` and `y_0=0` is the common zero dual value of the
outer/activated side, then:

1. the six points `y_0,...,y_5` are distinct and no three are collinear;
2. every edge `ij` of the complete graph `K_6` has a nonzero signed length
   `lambda_ij` with `|lambda_ij|=|y_i-y_j|`;
3. at every reciprocal vertex,
   \[
   \boxed{
   \sum_{j\ne i}{y_i-y_j\over\lambda_{ij}}=0;
   }
   \]
   each summand is a unit vector; and
4. the associated signed inverse-distance stress has stress-matrix rank at most three
   and satisfies the exact tensor cancellation
   \[
   \boxed{
   \sum_{i<j}{(y_i-y_j)(y_i-y_j)^T\over\lambda_{ij}}=0.
   }
   \]

Equivalently, a minimal `K_5` dual block would create a nondegenerate straight-line
`K_6` tensegrity in the reciprocal plane in which every one of the fifteen edges exerts
force of exactly unit magnitude and every reciprocal vertex is in equilibrium.

This is now a finite geometric target.  Excluding these reciprocal equilibria under the
additional cyclic-order data inherited from the five physical contact pentagons would
remove the sharp `K_5` corank block.  No such exclusion is claimed here.

---

## 1. The minimal degree-pure block

Let the five residual one-anchor faces be

\[
F_1,\ldots,F_5.
\]

Assume each is a bounded strict unit-contact pentagon and the nonparallel relation graph
on these faces is `K_5`.  Let

\[
y=(y_1,\ldots,y_5)\in\ker C_{face}^T
\]

be a nonzero dual mode supported on this component.  The one-anchor theorem gives

\[
y_i=c_i a_i,
\qquad c_i\ne0,
\tag{1.1}
\]

where `a_i` is the projective direction of the unique train from `F_i` to the
outer/activated side.

All activated dual face variables are zero, and the actual outer face has no bounded
closure block.  For this component they can therefore be represented by one reciprocal
point

\[
y_0:=0.
\tag{1.2}
\]

Each `F_i` has exactly five terminal train occurrences: its unique outside/activated
anchor and one internal train to each of the four other faces in the `K_5` block.

---

## 2. The five nonzero dual vectors are distinct

First, `y_i ne 0` by (1.1).

Suppose `y_i=y_j` for distinct `i,j`.  If some third `y_k` differs from this common
value, then

\[
y_k-y_i=y_k-y_j\ne0.
\]

The transpose equations on the relation edges `ki` and `kj` say these two nonzero
vectors are projectively parallel to the corresponding two train directions.  Thus the
pentagon `F_k` would contain two distinct projectively parallel sides, contradicting the
bounded-pentagon no-parallel-side theorem.

Therefore any coincidence `y_i=y_j` would force

\[
y_1=\cdots=y_5=:v\ne0.
\tag{2.1}
\]

But then all five outside-anchor directions are projectively parallel to `v`.  Sum the
five physical pentagon closure equations.  Every internal `F_iF_j` train occurs with
opposite oriented side vectors on its two terminal faces and cancels.  What remains is
the sum of the five oriented unit anchor-side vectors.  Under (2.1) each is either
`v/|v|` or `-v/|v|`.  A sum of five numbers from `{+1,-1}` cannot be zero, so the five
parallel unit vectors cannot close.  Contradiction.

Hence

\[
\boxed{y_1,\ldots,y_5\text{ are pairwise distinct and nonzero}.}
\tag{2.2}
\]

---

## 3. No three reciprocal points are collinear

The six points

\[
y_0,y_1,\ldots,y_5
\]

have no collinear triple.

If `y_i,y_j,y_k` with `i,j,k>0` were distinct and collinear, then at physical face
`F_i` the internal trains `ij` and `ik` would be projectively parallel, because their
dual differences `y_i-y_j` and `y_i-y_k` lie on the same line.  This is impossible for
a bounded contact pentagon.

If `y_0=0,y_i,y_j` were collinear, then at `F_i` its outside anchor train and the
internal train `ij` would be projectively parallel, again contradicting the pentagon
lemma.

Thus

\[
\boxed{\text{no three of }y_0,\ldots,y_5\text{ are collinear}.}
\tag{3.1}
\]

In particular the six reciprocal points affinely span the plane.

---

## 4. Signed reciprocal edge lengths

For an internal train between `F_i` and `F_j`, choose its coherent unit direction
`u_ij` and let the occurrence sign at `F_i` be `epsilon_ij`; the occurrence at `F_j`
has sign `-epsilon_ij`.  The dual train coefficient satisfies

\[
\epsilon_{ij}(y_i-y_j)=\lambda_\tau u_{ij}.
\]

The oriented physical side vector contributed by this train to the closure of `F_i`
is

\[
w_{ij}^{(i)}:=\epsilon_{ij}u_{ij}.
\]

Multiplying the dual relation by `epsilon_ij` gives

\[
\boxed{
y_i-y_j=\lambda_{ij}w_{ij}^{(i)},}
\tag{4.1}
\]

where `lambda_ij:=lambda_tau`.  At the other endpoint,

\[
w_{ij}^{(j)}=-w_{ij}^{(i)}
={y_j-y_i\over\lambda_{ij}}.
\]

For the unique outside/activated anchor of `F_i`, the same calculation with `y_0=0`
gives

\[
\boxed{
y_i-y_0=\lambda_{i0}w_{i0}^{(i)}.}
\tag{4.2}
\]

By Sections 2--3 all reciprocal edge differences are nonzero.  Every physical side
vector in (4.1)--(4.2) has unit length, hence

\[
\boxed{
\lambda_{ij}\ne0,
\qquad
|\lambda_{ij}|=|y_i-y_j|
\quad(0\le i<j\le5).
}
\tag{4.3}
\]

Thus all fifteen edges of the reciprocal complete graph are present with nonzero signed
length.

---

## 5. Unit-force equilibrium at all six reciprocal vertices

The physical pentagon closure equation at `F_i` is the sum of its five oriented unit
sides.  Using (4.1)--(4.2), it becomes

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{\substack{0\le j\le5\\j\ne i}}
{y_i-y_j\over\lambda_{ij}}=0,
\qquad i=1,\ldots,5.
}
\tag{5.1}
\]

Each summand has norm one by (4.3).

Now sum (5.1) over `i=1,...,5`.  Every internal term `ij` cancels against the term
`ji`.  The remaining anchor terms give

\[
\sum_{i=1}^5{y_i-y_0\over\lambda_{i0}}=0.
\]

Negating this identity gives the missing reciprocal equilibrium at `y_0`:

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j=1}^5{y_0-y_j\over\lambda_{0j}}=0.
}
\tag{5.2}
\]

Hence (5.1)--(5.2) hold for every `i=0,...,5`.

Put

\[
s_{ij}:=\operatorname{sgn}(\lambda_{ij}),
\qquad
r_{ij}:=|y_i-y_j|.
\]

Then

\[
{y_i-y_j\over\lambda_{ij}}
=s_{ij}{y_i-y_j\over r_{ij}},
\]

so the reciprocal system is exactly a complete straight-line framework with one unit
central force on every edge, attractive or repulsive according to `s_ij`, and zero net
force at every vertex.

---

## 6. Stress-matrix and tensor consequences

Define the symmetric signed inverse-distance edge weights

\[
\omega_{ij}:={1\over\lambda_{ij}}
={s_{ij}\over r_{ij}}.
\tag{6.1}
\]

Let `Omega` be the usual six-by-six stress matrix,

\[
\Omega_{ij}=-\omega_{ij}\quad(i\ne j),
\qquad
\Omega_{ii}=\sum_{j\ne i}\omega_{ij}.
\]

The reciprocal equilibrium equations give

\[
\Omega\mathbf1=0,
\qquad
\Omega Y_x=0,
\qquad
\Omega Y_y=0.
\tag{6.2}
\]

Because the reciprocal points affinely span the plane, the three displayed kernel
vectors are linearly independent.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}\Omega\le3.}
\tag{6.3}
\]

Equivalently every four-by-four minor of this signed inverse-distance stress matrix
vanishes.  This converts the minimal `K_5` obstruction into a finite algebraic system in
six planar points and fifteen edge signs.

There is also a coordinate-free second-moment identity.  Multiply the equilibrium at
`y_i` on the left by `y_i` and sum over all six vertices.  Pairing the two endpoint
contributions of each edge gives

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{0\le i<j\le5}
{(y_i-y_j)(y_i-y_j)^T\over\lambda_{ij}}=0.
}
\tag{6.4}
\]

Writing

\[
e_{ij}:={y_i-y_j\over r_{ij}},
\]

this is

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{i<j}s_{ij}r_{ij}\,e_{ij}e_{ij}^T=0.
}
\tag{6.5}
\]

So the positive-sign and negative-sign edge sets have exactly the same
length-weighted directional second moment:

\[
\sum_{s_{ij}=+1}r_{ij}e_{ij}e_{ij}^T
=
\sum_{s_{ij}=-1}r_{ij}e_{ij}e_{ij}^T.
\tag{6.6}
\]

Taking traces yields the exact signed length balance

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{s_{ij}=+1}r_{ij}
=
\sum_{s_{ij}=-1}r_{ij}.
}
\tag{6.7}
\]

Since `lambda_ij=s_ij r_ij`, (6.7) is also

\[
\sum_{i<j}\lambda_{ij}=0,
\]

the all-rotation dual identity specialized to this block.

---

## 7. Why this is a sharper finite target

The previous description of the minimal dual obstruction was combinatorial: a live
one-anchor relation component could have five vertices and relation graph `K_5`.  The
result above adds metric and equilibrium structure.

Any such minimal degree-pure block must produce six reciprocal points satisfying all of
the following simultaneously:

- no three reciprocal points are collinear;
- every one of the fifteen reciprocal edges carries nonzero stress;
- the magnitude of the force on every edge is exactly one;
- the signed inverse-distance stress matrix has rank at most three;
- the positive and negative reciprocal edges have identical length-weighted directional
  second moments; and
- at each of the five non-ground reciprocal vertices, the five unit force directions
  occur as the side directions of an actual strict bounded unit-contact pentagon in the
  source.

The last bullet retains source geometry that a generic six-point tensegrity would not
have.

This reduction does not prove that the reciprocal system is impossible.  It gives a
smaller next problem than an arbitrary `K_5` closure block: classify or exclude signed
six-point inverse-distance equilibria satisfying the inherited five pentagon cyclic
orders.  An exclusion would remove the support-size-five equality case in the
one-anchor corank theorem; a classification could instead expose an additional exact
train-angle flex or a source-level forbidden chord.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** converted every degree-pure minimal `K_5` one-anchor dual block into a
nondegenerate six-point reciprocal `K_6` with unit-magnitude edge forces, rank-at-most-
three inverse-distance stress matrix, and exact signed second-moment cancellation.
C-II remains open.